      "name": "SetInvalidStringIndex",
      "signature": "void SetInvalidStringIndex(ushort * pwIndex)",
      "calling_convention": "__d2regcall",
      "return_type": "void",
      "comment": "Sets a string table index to the invalid sentinel value 0xFFFF.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Write 0xFFFF (invalid index) to the location pointed to by the input pointer.\n2. Return immediately.\n\nParameters:\n- pwIndex (ushort*, IMPLICIT via EAX): Pointer to string index storage location.\n\nReturns:\n- void - The function modifies memory at the pointed location.\n\nMagic Numbers:\n- 0xFFFF: Invalid string index sentinel. Used to indicate \"no string\" or \"not found\" state in the string table lookup system. This matches common patterns where -1 (as unsigned 16-bit) indicates an uninitialized or invalid entry.\n\nUsage Context:\nThis function is used to initialize or reset string index variables to an invalid state. Called before string table lookups or when clearing cached string references. The sentinel value 0xFFFF ensures subsequent code can detect uninitialized indices.\n\nCalling Convention:\nUses implicit EAX register for the pointer parameter. The decompiler shows in_EAX because this function uses a non-standard register calling pattern where the first argument arrives in EAX rather than on the stack or in the calling convention-defined registers.",
